values ('galle', 'Galle', 'South Coast', 'A walled sea-port of ramparts and quiet lanes', 'Wander the ramparts, churches and boutiques of Galle Fort, a living colonial-era town on the southern coast and a UNESCO World Heritage Site.', array['First fortified by the Portuguese in the sixteenth century, Galle was extensively rebuilt by the Dutch in the seventeenth century, whose ramparts still encircle the old town today. The Old Town of Galle and its Fortifications was inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1988.', 'Inside the walls, narrow streets of low colonial houses hold cafés, galleries and small hotels alongside homes, schools and places of worship. The ramparts, the white lighthouse and the clock tower are at their most atmospheric in the late afternoon.', 'Galle makes a relaxed base for the beaches of the south-west coast, including Unawatuna, and is around two hours from Colombo via the Southern Expressway.']::text[], '[{"title":"Sunset on the ramparts","text":"Walk the walls from the clock tower to the lighthouse as the sun drops into the Indian Ocean."},{"title":"Fort lanes and architecture","text":"Colonial churches, merchant houses and courtyards, best explored slowly on foot."},{"title":"Galle Lighthouse","text":"The white lighthouse on the south-eastern corner of the fort, framed by palms and sea."},{"title":"Nearby beaches","text":"Unawatuna and the coves along the coast are a short drive away."}]'::jsonb, 'December to April usually brings the calmest seas and sunniest weather on the south-west coast. May to September can be wetter with rougher seas.', 'Around 2 hours from Colombo and roughly 2.5 hours from the airport via the Southern Expressway.', array['culture', 'beach', 'food']::public.interest[], 'galleClockTower', array['galleLighthouse', 'mirissaShore']::text[], 6.0261, 80.217, 3, true)
